# Clean Notes

**Meeting notes that survive a loud room.**

Every voice AI demo is recorded in a quiet office. Real conversations happen in cafés, lobbies and
co-working spaces — and that's where transcription quietly falls apart. Not by producing gibberish you'd
notice, but by producing clean, plausible, *incomplete* notes. Sentences go missing. Nobody checks,
because the output looks fine.

This records a conversation, runs it through a speech enhancement model before transcription, and shows
you both versions side by side. Same audio, same transcriber, same cleanup prompt. One passes through a
denoiser first.

Built in one day at the 8x Mobile Hack, Berlin.

---

## The result

Enhancement doesn't make the best case better. It removes the worst case.

| | mean WER | spread across runs | notes length |
|---|---|---|---|
| Raw | 30.1% | **8.0%** | 1326–1622 chars |
| Enhanced | 27.0% | **1.9%** | 1522–1628 chars |

Three transcription passes over the same 2:44 recording at 3 dB SNR.

The mean improves by 3.2 points, which is real but modest. The interesting number is the spread. Raw
transcription is *unstable* — the same audio, transcribed three times, ranged over eight points and on
its worst run silently dropped ~300 characters of the conversation. Enhanced stayed within a two-point
band and never collapsed.

For meeting notes that's the difference that matters. You don't get to re-run a meeting.

Honest caveats, since they change how you should read the table: this is a small sample on synthetic
audio at a friendly SNR, `gpt-4o-transcribe` is non-deterministic, and enhancement narrows the gap
without closing it — some turns are lost on both paths.

---

## Architecture

```
iPhone microphone
    │  AVAudioRecorder — .playAndRecord, .default mode, no voice processing
    │  16 kHz mono m4a
    ▼
base64 chunks over WebSocket
    │
    ▼
ffmpeg decode → 16 kHz mono PCM
    │
    ├─ raw ──────────────────────────┐
    │                                 │
    └─ DPDFNet (dpdfnet4) ────────┐   │
                                  ▼   ▼
                          gpt-4o-transcribe
                                  │
                                  ▼
                        LLM cleanup (identical prompt)
                                  │
                                  ▼
                     RAW notes  │  ENHANCED notes
```

**Nothing else touches the audio.** No platform voice processing, no AGC, no noise suppression on either
path — verified by reading `expo-audio`'s iOS sources. The only difference between the two paths is
DPDFNet. That's what makes the comparison honest.

### Two decisions worth explaining

**The raw path runs concurrently with enhancement.** Raw transcription doesn't need the denoised audio,
so it overlaps the 61-second enhance instead of queueing behind it — and its notes are pushed to the
phone the moment they're ready, roughly 60 seconds before the enhanced ones. You watch the raw notes
arrive, read them, and then see what was missing.

**Enhancement runs on the laptop, not the phone.** DPDFNet is causal and could stream, but on-device
means converting a model with persistent recurrent state to CoreML — weeks of work, not hours. Over
local wifi the round trip is invisible.

---

## Performance

Measured on a 2:44 recording:

| Stage | Time |
|---|---|
| Enhancement (DPDFNet, RTF 0.37) | 61 s |
| Raw transcribe + cleanup | overlapped, free |
| Enhanced transcribe + cleanup | 13 s |
| **Total round trip** | **74 s** |
| Raw notes on screen | 13 s |

---

## Stack

- **Phone** — Expo SDK 54, React Native, TypeScript, `expo-audio`
- **Server** — FastAPI, WebSocket, ffmpeg
- **Enhancement** — [DPDFNet](https://github.com/ceva-ip/DPDFNet) (Apache-2.0), PyTorch, 16 kHz
- **Transcription and cleanup** — OpenAI

---

## Running it

```bash
pip install torch torchaudio soundfile numpy jiwer fastapi uvicorn
git clone https://github.com/ceva-ip/DPDFNet
# weights: huggingface.co/Ceva-IP/DPDFNet  → dpdfnet4, 16 kHz

echo "OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-..." > .env
python server.py          # prints the LAN URL it binds to
```

```bash
npx expo start            # set SERVER_URL in App.tsx to that LAN address
```

Benchmark against your own recordings:

```bash
python bench.py           # WER raw vs enhanced, per clip and mean
```

---

## What we learned

**The failure mode isn't garbled text.** We expected noisy audio to produce obvious nonsense. It
mostly produces confident, well-formed sentences with content quietly missing — which is far worse,
because nothing signals that you should check.

**One sample is a coin flip.** Our first side-by-side looked dramatic. Repeating it three times showed
the run-to-run variance of the transcriber was larger than the effect we were measuring. The reliability
story only appeared once we stopped trusting a single run.

**Most of the day went on the microphone, not the model.** A recording that reported a valid duration
still produced a file with no `moov` atom, because iOS hadn't finished writing the container when we read
it. The device now validates duration and file settlement before uploading, so a dead recording fails on
the phone in a second instead of on the server ninety seconds later.

---

## Next

- Real recordings in real rooms — the numbers above are synthetic and friendly
- On-device enhancement via CoreML, for a pipeline with no server at all
- Streaming rather than clip-based, now that RTF < 1 is confirmed